WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.707
Weak passwords and poor recovery flows make credential attacks easier.

2
00:00:04.707 --> 00:00:14.458
Password policy, breached-password screening, recovery verification, and secure reset processes all contribute to the strength of authentication.

3
00:00:14.458 --> 00:00:22.797
Multifactor authentication reduces account takeover risk, but only when it is enforced consistently and resistant to bypass.

4
00:00:22.797 --> 00:00:29.320
Enrollment, recovery, administrative exceptions, and remembered-device logic need careful review.

5
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:35.439
Session management determines whether authenticated access remains trustworthy after login.

6
00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:45.862
Session tokens should be unpredictable, protected in transit and storage, rotated at appropriate times, and invalidated on logout or major account changes.

7
00:00:45.862 --> 00:00:56.218
Authentication controls must account for automated attack patterns such as credential stuffing, password spraying, brute force attempts, and token replay.

8
00:00:56.218 --> 00:01:00.051
Require MFA for sensitive access and high-risk workflows.

9
00:01:00.051 --> 00:01:04.019
Use strong password and authenticator management practices.

10
00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:09.264
Protect sessions with secure token handling, expiration, and reauthentication.

11
00:01:09.264 --> 00:01:19.755
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include IA-2 - Identification and Authentication; IA-5 - Authenticator Management; AC-7 - Unsuccessful Logon Attempts.

